00:00:49.809 --> 00:00:51.992
- [Voiceover] Thomas asked, "Hey Gary,

00:00:51.992 --> 00:00:53.685
"what is the future of
blogging in all this

00:00:53.685 --> 00:00:56.523
"rented social media space?"

00:00:56.983 --> 00:00:59.723
- You know Thomas, I think the
interesting thing about blogs

00:00:59.723 --> 00:01:04.657
and it's really fascinating for
me to watch what's happening

00:01:04.657 --> 00:01:08.726
is in 2003, four, five, six,

00:01:08.726 --> 00:01:13.468
it was very commonplace to
use the internet in a way

00:01:13.468 --> 00:01:16.692
where you would be driving
someone to your home, right?

00:01:16.696 --> 00:01:20.620
Like, come here to consume me here.

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:22.431
I think what we're seeing a lot now is

00:01:22.431 --> 00:01:24.289
I'm over here.

00:01:24.289 --> 00:01:25.845
If you want me to know what's up,

00:01:25.845 --> 00:01:28.166
you better learn how to speak to me here.

00:01:28.166 --> 00:01:30.925
And so I think that what's happened is

00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:32.972
the attention graph that
I spend so much time

00:01:32.972 --> 00:01:36.478
paying attention to is shifting,

00:01:36.478 --> 00:01:39.892
and people's willingness
to jump to some place

00:01:39.892 --> 00:01:42.957
to consume is not down
because the truth is

00:01:42.957 --> 00:01:46.833
I believe social networks,
specifically Twitter and Facebook

00:01:46.833 --> 00:01:51.293
and Pinterest, yeah those, LinkedIn,

00:01:51.293 --> 00:01:54.008
are really gateway drug pieces of content

00:01:54.008 --> 00:01:57.167
to drive to this before mentioned home,

00:01:57.167 --> 00:01:59.763
a blog, an e-commerce site,

00:01:59.763 --> 00:02:02.594
your download page to your app.

00:02:02.594 --> 00:02:06.478
So, I think what's changed
is people understanding

00:02:06.478 --> 00:02:10.216
they need a great skill to
be a wonderful storyteller

00:02:10.216 --> 00:02:14.395
within social networks to
become a first impression

00:02:14.395 --> 00:02:15.858
to the, ya know, it's like
you have to be really good

00:02:15.858 --> 00:02:19.179
at the bar before you get a
chance to do something at home.

00:02:19.179 --> 00:02:22.036
And I think that's the
analogy that I'm looking for,

00:02:22.036 --> 00:02:24.363
if you know what I mean.

00:02:24.363 --> 00:02:25.818
- [Voiceover] John asked,
"What are your thoughts

00:02:25.818 --> 00:02:27.722
"on creating a successful, long term

00:02:27.722 --> 00:02:30.672
"social media strategy for
yourself or your clients?

00:02:30.672 --> 00:02:32.042
"How long in advance do you create

00:02:32.042 --> 00:02:34.507
"the content you roll out?"

00:02:37.149 --> 00:02:38.102
- John, that's a good question.

00:02:38.102 --> 00:02:40.708
I mean, I think this all comes down to

00:02:40.708 --> 00:02:42.583
something that I call reverse engineering.

00:02:42.583 --> 00:02:45.187
The truth is everybody's different.

00:02:45.187 --> 00:02:49.803
You know, my vision is very long-term.

00:02:49.803 --> 00:02:52.781
I don't know how you define
long-term in your question,

00:02:52.781 --> 00:02:55.748
but some people think long-term
is three to five years.

00:02:55.748 --> 00:02:59.001
I think long-term is until the day I die.

00:02:59.001 --> 00:03:02.476
And so, my clients may not be as patient

00:03:02.476 --> 00:03:06.768
when your Fortune 500 company that needs

00:03:06.768 --> 00:03:09.902
to hit numbers each quarter, your patience

00:03:09.902 --> 00:03:13.404
to build a three to five
year plan is nonexistent.

00:03:13.404 --> 00:03:16.126
When you're a Series C Startup company

00:03:16.126 --> 00:03:18.531
that just raised 200 million dollars,

00:03:18.531 --> 00:03:21.489
and you're only burning
four million dollars,

00:03:21.489 --> 00:03:23.160
you've got a lot of patience, and the idea

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.872
of building brand and
having a patience game

00:03:26.872 --> 00:03:29.963
to your execution becomes more attractive,

00:03:29.963 --> 00:03:31.565
then we reverse engineer that.

00:03:31.565 --> 00:03:34.166
Then it's more about branding, Instagram,

00:03:34.166 --> 00:03:37.115
doing high-end video, long-form content

00:03:37.115 --> 00:03:39.902
with no right hook, lot of jabbing.

00:03:39.902 --> 00:03:41.341
If you're a startup that's
gonna go out of business

00:03:41.341 --> 00:03:44.267
in 24 weeks if you don't sell some stuff,

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:48.471
we're in full right hook,
ya know, Facebook dark post,

00:03:48.471 --> 00:03:50.770
SEO, SEM, influencer marketing

00:03:50.770 --> 00:03:53.877
with calls to action to sell.

00:03:53.877 --> 00:03:58.195
Ya know, all that stuff
completely is determined based on

00:03:58.195 --> 00:04:02.574
the client's current
short-term and long-term needs,

00:04:02.574 --> 00:04:05.605
but the truth is short-term
and long-term needs

00:04:05.605 --> 00:04:09.175
really balance based on a moment in time,

00:04:09.175 --> 00:04:12.861
and so, ya know,

00:04:12.861 --> 00:04:15.724
the reason I think I'm
good at business is,

00:04:15.724 --> 00:04:17.809
for all of my talking,

00:04:17.809 --> 00:04:21.776
I am 10X at my listening skills,

00:04:21.776 --> 00:04:23.762
and it all just comes down to listening,

00:04:23.762 --> 00:04:27.222
and so the way we strategize
is predicated on listening,

00:04:27.222 --> 00:04:29.335
and I think the biggest
challenge for so many

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:32.515
of the VaynerNation
that's watching right now

00:04:32.515 --> 00:04:35.530
is I don't think a lot
of you, and this is,

00:04:35.530 --> 00:04:37.554
with all due respect,
this is for everybody,

00:04:37.554 --> 00:04:40.480
I'm just picking on you
'cause I love you, tough love.

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.570
I think a lot of people aren't really sure

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.446
what they want to accomplish
in a one-year window

00:04:47.446 --> 00:04:49.582
versus a five-year window
versus a ten-year window,

00:04:49.582 --> 00:04:53.017
and their behavior doesn't map to it.

00:04:53.017 --> 00:04:54.666
Ya know, to me I got lucky.

00:04:54.666 --> 00:04:58.078
I just decided it's everybody
shows up to my funeral,

00:04:58.078 --> 00:05:01.888
hedge forever, build up equity,
cash it in as I need it,

00:05:01.888 --> 00:05:05.634
if I ever need it, which has
allowed me to be very patient

00:05:05.634 --> 00:05:09.967
and really has allowed
me to dictate my behavior

00:05:09.967 --> 00:05:12.382
being probably a better human being.

00:05:12.382 --> 00:05:14.473
And in a weird way, and
again, I think a lot of people

00:05:14.473 --> 00:05:15.935
would find this funny.

00:05:15.935 --> 00:05:18.126
In a lot of ways, I've been a pushover

00:05:18.126 --> 00:05:21.878
as a entrepreneur

00:05:21.878 --> 00:05:23.783
because if you would look
at it in the short-term,

00:05:23.783 --> 00:05:25.525
I'm leaving money on the table.

00:05:25.525 --> 00:05:27.544
I'm not fighting for every cent.

00:05:27.544 --> 00:05:30.679
I'm not trying to drill it
down to the biggest advantage.

00:05:30.679 --> 00:05:33.489
I'm not even getting mine everytime

00:05:33.489 --> 00:05:35.393
because I'm just hedging along the way,

00:05:35.393 --> 00:05:38.550
and so just comes down to what
you're trying to accomplish.

00:05:38.550 --> 00:05:42.427
I think the better question
to this question is

00:05:42.427 --> 00:05:45.958
how can you help someone
or are you capable

00:05:45.958 --> 00:05:49.411
of really understanding what
you're trying to accomplish?

00:05:49.411 --> 00:05:50.990
- [Voiceover] Matthew asked, "Hey Gary,

00:05:50.990 --> 00:05:52.987
"I'm speaking to parents
at a public school event

00:05:52.987 --> 00:05:54.984
"on parenting in a social media world.

00:05:54.984 --> 00:05:57.117
"What would you teach them?"

00:05:58.397 --> 00:05:59.535
- Every time I get in front of parents,

00:05:59.535 --> 00:06:02.136
they get really upset
with me because my move is

00:06:02.136 --> 00:06:04.629
to punch them directly in the mouth.

00:06:04.629 --> 00:06:08.693
Parents are becoming their
parents on an everyday basis.

00:06:08.693 --> 00:06:11.784
I would tell them that
they're fearing technology

00:06:11.784 --> 00:06:14.209
and they're forcing their
kids to spend less time on it,

00:06:14.209 --> 00:06:15.394
which is not preparing them

00:06:15.394 --> 00:06:17.576
for the actual world in the future.

00:06:17.576 --> 00:06:19.363
I would tell them that they talk about

00:06:19.363 --> 00:06:22.683
how much they hate technology
and its impact on kids,

00:06:22.683 --> 00:06:24.505
but they should look
themselves in the mirror

00:06:24.505 --> 00:06:26.469
because every time they
don't wanna parent,

00:06:26.469 --> 00:06:28.652
or the kid's crying a little
bit more than they like,

00:06:28.652 --> 00:06:33.308
they throw an iPad at them
like it's a Chinese star.

00:06:33.308 --> 00:06:36.662
I would tell them that they're
full of shit and hypocrites

00:06:36.662 --> 00:06:39.238
and should never try to
build their self-esteem

00:06:39.238 --> 00:06:41.306
through their kids' accomplishments.

00:06:41.306 --> 00:06:43.159
If your little Ricky is
the best baseball player

00:06:43.159 --> 00:06:46.257
in the fourth grade team, that
has nothing to do with you,

00:06:46.257 --> 00:06:48.522
except it has your DNA, but
like don't walk around like

00:06:48.522 --> 00:06:52.423
you're the best player
on the fourth grade team.

00:06:52.423 --> 00:06:55.162
So, you know, I would tell them that

00:06:55.162 --> 00:06:56.857
it's never been a better
time to be a parent

00:06:56.857 --> 00:06:59.226
because all the social networking tools

00:06:59.226 --> 00:07:01.734
allow us to spy on our kids
and know what they're doing

00:07:01.734 --> 00:07:03.777
at a level we've never seen before.

00:07:03.777 --> 00:07:05.565
I'm, you know, I used
to think it was great

00:07:05.565 --> 00:07:07.237
that I lived in New York,
that I wouldn't have to worry

00:07:07.237 --> 00:07:08.305
about drunk driving.

00:07:08.305 --> 00:07:09.743
Now no parent does because

00:07:09.743 --> 00:07:11.695
by the time their kids are of age,

00:07:11.695 --> 00:07:14.349
Uber will be at scale.

00:07:14.349 --> 00:07:16.082
I would tell them to stop playing defense

00:07:16.082 --> 00:07:18.637
and stop being scared
and start playing offense

00:07:18.637 --> 00:07:20.750
and start being pumped.

00:07:20.750 --> 00:07:22.167
- [Voiceover] Moveandbezel asked,

00:07:22.167 --> 00:07:23.814
"We are in a nonexistent market,

00:07:23.814 --> 00:07:26.462
"but the sharing economy is
growing, and we play in there.

00:07:26.462 --> 00:07:28.204
"What would you do to
determine or come up with

00:07:28.204 --> 00:07:30.256
"a market size?"

00:07:31.216 --> 00:07:34.402
- Moveandbezel, you know,
obviously I've jammed with you

00:07:34.402 --> 00:07:36.608
a little bit on social.

00:07:36.608 --> 00:07:39.558
Ya know, I guess you're
asking me this question

00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:42.587
to try to figure out how
to put it into your deck

00:07:42.587 --> 00:07:44.395
so that you can raise money.

00:07:44.395 --> 00:07:46.104
You know, the people that
are gonna give you money

00:07:46.104 --> 00:07:48.776
are not gonna give you money
on the potential market size

00:07:48.776 --> 00:07:51.490
of the watch renting market.

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:54.719
They're gonna do it because,
you're right, it doesn't exist.

00:07:54.719 --> 00:07:56.624
They're gonna do it
because they believe in you

00:07:56.624 --> 00:07:58.737
as an entrepreneur hustler,

00:07:58.737 --> 00:08:02.033
and they're gonna believe
in the sharing economy,

00:08:02.033 --> 00:08:06.027
and they're gonna think that
watches are an interest graph

00:08:06.027 --> 00:08:08.464
of end users, but ya know,

00:08:08.464 --> 00:08:11.912
I don't think you have
to come up with, ya know,

00:08:11.912 --> 00:08:14.456
the amount of financial
engineering that goes on

00:08:14.456 --> 00:08:17.451
in startup culture and the
finance world and raising capital

00:08:17.451 --> 00:08:18.541
is really silly to me.

00:08:18.541 --> 00:08:20.307
What you could do is you could find out

00:08:20.307 --> 00:08:22.838
how big the AirBnB market
is, then you could figure out

00:08:22.838 --> 00:08:24.440
how big the renting market is

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:26.437
versus the overall watch market,

00:08:26.437 --> 00:08:29.266
and then you could just do
the math and say, "Oh OK."

00:08:29.266 --> 00:08:33.217
There's a lot of hacking you can do,

00:08:33.217 --> 00:08:34.934
but I think you're asking
the wrong question,

00:08:34.934 --> 00:08:39.479
and I think India, when you
asked it before we did the show,

00:08:39.479 --> 00:08:40.716
I think, wasn't there also like

00:08:40.716 --> 00:08:43.077
"How do you market to a market
that doesn't exist yet?" or something?

00:08:43.077 --> 00:08:43.943
- [India] I think that
would be interesting.

00:08:43.943 --> 00:08:47.914
- Ya know, I do too, and
I think that, ya know,

00:08:47.914 --> 00:08:51.862
I think that I've spent a lot
of time thinking about that

00:08:51.862 --> 00:08:53.533
in general in my career,

00:08:53.533 --> 00:08:56.552
and I dislike it when
people that are started

00:08:56.552 --> 00:08:59.524
or businesses in it are
asking that question

00:08:59.524 --> 00:09:01.422
because obviously you've
started the business

00:09:01.422 --> 00:09:03.930
with a belief that that
market's gonna happen,

00:09:03.930 --> 00:09:06.987
and more importantly, I think
there's a confusion to you

00:09:06.987 --> 00:09:09.897
about what you're actually marketing to.

00:09:09.897 --> 00:09:11.847
You're thinking, "How
do I market to people

00:09:11.847 --> 00:09:13.844
"to build awareness
that, hey, you can trade

00:09:13.844 --> 00:09:16.002
"and rent and share watches."

00:09:16.002 --> 00:09:18.697
The truth is there's
just watch enthusiasts.

00:09:18.697 --> 00:09:20.112
And so like, they're there.

00:09:20.112 --> 00:09:23.038
Marketing to, ya know, I
think people get confused

00:09:23.038 --> 00:09:25.268
when they're marketing a new version

00:09:25.268 --> 00:09:28.124
of a historical product
versus creating a new product

00:09:28.124 --> 00:09:30.501
that doesn't exist, ya know.

00:09:30.501 --> 00:09:32.976
Uber didn't have to sell that.

00:09:32.976 --> 00:09:36.018
Like, people rented black
cars and took taxis.

00:09:36.018 --> 00:09:39.614
They had to bring some
awareness to they existed,

00:09:39.614 --> 00:09:40.731
but I don't think anybody's confused

00:09:40.731 --> 00:09:42.102
that's listening or watching right now

00:09:42.102 --> 00:09:43.963
or of anybody out here
they're like, "Oh, watches?"

00:09:43.963 --> 00:09:45.695
Ya know, India, there's watches.

00:09:45.695 --> 00:09:48.365
You can rent them and you
can put yours on there.

00:09:48.365 --> 00:09:50.385
Like, it's just not super complicated.

00:09:50.385 --> 00:09:53.357
Ya know, just not complicated, and so

00:09:53.357 --> 00:09:56.375
you market to people that
give a crap about watches.

00:09:56.375 --> 00:09:58.164
- [Voiceover] Andrew
asked, "My investor wants

00:09:58.164 --> 00:10:02.000
"to change the name of my
company's brand Sasquatch Fuel.

00:10:02.000 --> 00:10:03.434
"Should I change the name to something

00:10:03.434 --> 00:10:07.261
"that includes the name of our
unique pouch like Omni Fuel?"

00:10:09.541 --> 00:10:10.493
- Andrew, I think a lot of people

00:10:10.493 --> 00:10:12.025
that are watching this show are gonna say,

00:10:12.025 --> 00:10:13.488
"He's about to go crazy and be like

00:10:13.488 --> 00:10:15.794
"screw your investors,
stick to your guns."

00:10:15.794 --> 00:10:19.409
Truth is I'm a very funny
guy when it comes to names.

00:10:19.409 --> 00:10:21.312
I think execution is everything,

00:10:21.312 --> 00:10:24.065
and I think the name is
literally irrelevant.

00:10:24.065 --> 00:10:26.818
Like, ya know, what did
Google mean to anybody

00:10:26.818 --> 00:10:28.720
before, ya know, it became something?

00:10:28.720 --> 00:10:31.274
What did the word Nike mean to anybody

00:10:31.274 --> 00:10:33.502
before Nike made it happen?

00:10:33.502 --> 00:10:36.358
Like, my last name is Vaynerchuk, ya know?

00:10:36.358 --> 00:10:38.262
Like they used to teach you in Hollywood

00:10:38.262 --> 00:10:41.264
before you build a brand, like
I would've been Gary Smith

00:10:41.264 --> 00:10:45.042
ya know in 1961 if I went
to Hollywood and did this.

00:10:45.042 --> 00:10:47.690
Like, names don't mean crap.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:51.521
What you make that name
mean is the real game.

00:10:51.521 --> 00:10:55.213
And so, ya know, you wanna be a pushover?

00:10:55.213 --> 00:10:55.909
I'm just kidding.

00:10:55.909 --> 00:10:58.534
You want to change your name? Great.

00:10:58.534 --> 00:11:00.320
You want like, you don't
want to change your name?

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:01.133
Great.

00:11:01.133 --> 00:11:02.620
Bottom line is is your product good?

00:11:02.620 --> 00:11:04.291
Are you gonna be able to market?

00:11:04.291 --> 00:11:06.149
I mean, ya know, what's the other name

00:11:06.149 --> 00:11:07.496
that he's considering changing it to?

00:11:07.496 --> 00:11:10.471
- [India] He said they have a,
their pouch is called Omni--

00:11:10.471 --> 00:11:12.266
- Yeah, I like Sasquatch better than Omni.

00:11:12.266 --> 00:11:13.881
Something I remember better than Omni.

00:11:13.881 --> 00:11:15.576
Like, everything's Omni.

00:11:15.576 --> 00:11:18.639
And so yeah, that's it.

00:11:18.639 --> 00:11:20.267
That's all I got.

